            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Total, irreversible destruction, permanent judgment, and complete obliteration, indicating that an entity (such as Babylon or an unrepentant sinner) will be violently removed and never seen again.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 18:21\",\"Matthew 18:6\",\"Jeremiah 51:63-64\",\"Mark 9:42\",\"Luke 17:2\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The complete removal and forgiveness of sins, hiding them forever from God's sight.\",\"basis\":[\"Micah 7:19\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The overcoming of seemingly impossible obstacles through the exercise of unwavering faith.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 21:21\",\"Mark 11:23\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Apocalyptic judgment striking the chaotic Gentile nations or the abyss, often represented symbolically by the sea.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 8:8\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The motif of a heavy object (especially a stone or millstone) sinking into the sea is a consistent biblical metaphor for a violent, irreversible end. This imagery heavily features in prophetic literature concerning the fall of Babylon, as well as Jesus' warnings about the severe consequences of leading others into sin.\"}",
